// How to size your memory: build with -profile memory, run, read memory.txt.
//
// A partial array, `[N]T`, always holds room for N; an arena, its whole
// size. `mara build memory_profiling -profile memory` makes a program that
// watches how full each one gets. When it exits (main's end, an assert, a
// crash) it writes memory.txt next to the exe: one line per place an N is
// written — a local's declaration, a struct's field — the most room it
// could give back first, then one line per arena.
//
// The columns:
//   unused B    what each instance would save with N its peak: where to look
//   reserved B  the room each instance holds: N times an element's bytes
//   peak        the most any instance ever held (its len at its fullest)
//   median      the middle of the instances' peaks: a typical one
//   cap         N
//   elem B      an element's bytes
//   instances   how many there were: a fun's local, once per call; a
//               struct's field, once per struct made (or copied in)
//   name        `fun.local` or `Struct.field`; an array's elements, `name[]`
//   where       the file and line it's written at
// And for each arena: its size, the most of it in use at once, and that as a
// percent.
//
// Set N above the peak, not at it: the run you profiled may not be the
// fullest one. Every len the program writes is looked at, so a -profile
// memory build runs slower; build without it to time things (see
// profiling.mara).
module memory_profiling

use mara.core
use mara.memory

#allocator Arena_Basic(1 * MB)

// Each one names an item: its name fills part of its 64.
Item :: struct {
	name: [64]utf8
	count: i64
}

// A local, once per call: it holds n.
squares :: fun(n: i64) -> i64 {
	found : [1000]i64
	for k in 0 .. n {
		&found + k * k
	}
	return found.len
}

main :: fun() {
	total := 0
	for n in 1 .. 50 {
		total += squares(n)    // squares.found: peak 49 of 1000
	}

	items : [100]Item
	for k in 0 .. 3 {
		it : Item
		it.name = "an item"    // Item.name: peak 7 of 64
		it.count = k
		&items + it
	}

	print(total, items.len)    // main.items: peak 3 of 100
}
